# Store and check out every file byte-for-byte — no line-ending conversion.
#
# This is deliberate and load-bearing. The static-site publisher controls the
# exact bytes of every page it writes: it opens files with newline="" and
# deliberately emits CRLF at three specific seams (see _wrap_report and _footer
# in intelligence/publish.py) to match the reference markup. A published report
# is therefore legitimately mixed — a handful of CRLF among hundreds of LF.
#
# Git's default "* text=auto" would normalise all of that to LF on commit and
# convert back to native endings on checkout. The Linux machine that runs the
# weekly update would then check out files that differ from what the publisher
# produces, so every page would look modified on every run: constant churn in
# the history, and committed content that no longer matches the app's output.
#
# "-text" disables the conversion entirely, which is what a project whose
# generator owns its bytes actually wants.
* -text
